Why can self-invocation bypass Spring AOP?

Because Spring AOP works through a proxy, and self-invocation does not go through that proxy.

Example:

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    public void processPayment() {
        validatePayment();   // self-invocation
    }

    @Transactional
    public void validatePayment() {
        // DB operation
    }
}

When another bean calls:

paymentService.validatePayment();

the call is actually:

Caller
  ↓
Spring Proxy
  ↓
@Transactional interceptor
  ↓
validatePayment()

So the transaction is applied.

But when processPayment() calls:

validatePayment();

the flow is:

Caller
  ↓
Spring Proxy
  ↓
processPayment()
  ↓
this.validatePayment()

The call goes directly to the same object (this), not back through the Spring proxy.

Therefore:

@Transactional
@Async
@Cacheable
@Retry
@PreAuthorize

and other proxy-based AOP advice may be bypassed.

Common solution

Move the advised method to another Spring bean:

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    private final ValidationService validationService;

    public PaymentService(ValidationService validationService) {
        this.validationService = validationService;
    }

    public void processPayment() {
        validationService.validatePayment();
    }
}
@Service
public class ValidationService {

    @Transactional
    public void validatePayment() {
        // DB operation
    }
}

Now:

PaymentService
      ↓
Spring Proxy of ValidationService
      ↓
@Transactional
      ↓
validatePayment()
Interview answer

Spring AOP is proxy-based. Self-invocation calls the method directly through this, so the Spring proxy and its interceptors are bypassed.
Therefore annotations like @Transactional, @Async, @Cacheable, and @Retry may not take effect on self-invoked methods.